WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:19.979
In 1703, an anonymous Englishman, known to history only as Mr. C,

00:00:19.979 --> 00:00:23.540
wrote to the Royal Society of London

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:30.440
to report on a peculiar
observation he’d made using only a simple microscope.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:38.340
He’d been looking at the roots of pond-weed,
but as he looked closer, he found attached

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:47.250
to the roots, “‘many pretty branches, compos’d of rectangular oblongs and exact squares.’”

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:56.300
At first, he assumed these geometric attachments must be salt crystals.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.580
But the more he experimented and looked through
his microscope, the more he realized there

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.420
might be something amazing going on here.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.280
These tiny, beautiful shapes seemed kind of plant-like.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:19.180
Today, with the benefit of many, many more observations, and far superior equipment,

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.520
we know that this 18th century

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:29.080
letter is one of the earliest descriptions
of a diatom, a photosynthetic, unicellular

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:36.390
algae that can become so plentiful that oceanic
blooms of these organisms, which we cannot

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:42.840
see individually without a microscope, are
nonetheless visible from space.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.840
But, we wouldn’t be talking about diatoms
if we needed to be in space to observe them.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:01.840
You can find these tiny organisms just about
anywhere that has water and light.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.110
Looking at them through a microscope, you
might understand why microbe hunters are so

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:07.780
fond of them.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.049
They have been called “the jewels of the
sea”.

00:02:12.049 --> 00:02:19.750
Those beautiful outer shells are called frustules,
and they set diatoms apart from every other

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.760
living creature.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:28.519
Unlike the organic cell walls and membranes
we associate with most cells, frustules are

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:38.540
made out of inorganic silica, enclosing the cytoplasm of the diatom in, what is basically, glass.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:45.739
Silica shells take less energy to make and
maintain compared to their organic counterparts,

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:48.329
but they do come with a trade-off.

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:50.840
Glass is…well, it’s glass.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:57.620
It’s hard to expand if you’re a unicellular
organism trying to undergo asexual mitosis

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.549
when your cell is inclosed in a rigid, inorganic material.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:10.499
Instead, when diatoms divide, the daughter
cells take the old frustule and divide it

00:03:10.499 --> 00:03:16.779
between them, which means that the daughter
cells are both going to be smaller than their

00:03:16.779 --> 00:03:21.799
parent, and they’re never going to grow
any bigger.

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:28.060
And as diatoms keep dividing, the daughter
cells keep getting smaller and smaller.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:37.290
If this goes on forever, the diatom will get
so small that it cannot survive.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:43.569
But diatoms that are starting to get too small
can avoid that fate through sexual reproduction,

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:49.239
which is kind of a refresh button that lets
them construct a new frustule for a larger

00:03:49.239 --> 00:03:51.809
daughter cell.

00:03:51.809 --> 00:03:58.879
For the most part, diatoms can’t actually
move—they just go wherever the water takes them.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:06.920
But some diatoms are able to glide along a surface using a slit in their frustule called a raphe.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:11.299
This allows them to secrete
mucus that sticks to a surface but,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:15.180
how does that mucus help them move?

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.100
We...don’t know.

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:22.220
We can see that they move, so they must be able to

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:26.800
And we can see that mucus is always left behind when they do it,

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.150
but we don't know the exact mechanism of how it works.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.479
You and I, of course, move using muscles.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.460
And the moluecules responsible for that in us are actin and myosin.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:46.780
Here’s a wild thing, in 1999 some scientists put actin disrupting compounds

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:54.490
in solution with diatoms and those diatoms lost their ability to move.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:05:01.150
So while diatom motility remains something
of a mystery, these tiny, jewel-encrusted

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:08.830
algae move using the same molecular systems as us.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.940
If you've been looking closely,

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.320
you might be wondering what these bubbles are,

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.199
and if you are we hope we can surprise
you.

00:05:19.199 --> 00:05:25.259
Those are oil droplets, and they store energy for the diatom when they might be having

00:05:25.259 --> 00:05:29.699
trouble finding light or their usual nutrients.

00:05:29.699 --> 00:05:36.569
These little organisms are so good at creating
this fatty oil that scientists have wondered

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:43.600
whether we might use diatoms as tiny factories
that turn sunlight and carbondioxide into

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:49.080
fuel not just for them, but for our future
airplanes.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:57.100
It might be tempting to think of these cells just as little microscopic jewels, just something

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:03.440
nice to look at, but they also have a huge impact on our world.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:12.960
Of all of the photosynthesis being done on Earth, around 1/5 is done by diatoms, which puts

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.980
them on par with all rainforests on earth combined.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:23.640
and it means we owe a great deal to these microorganisms.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.500
And not just our oxygen,

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:32.520
when they die, their silica frustules
sink to the bottom of the water that they're in

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:37.890
and accumulate into a soft, chalky rock that
we know better diatomaceous earth.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:47.940
That key ingredient in beer and wine filtration,
paint, and of course, cat litter.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:56.500
So, yes, these beatufiul jewels that the unknown Mr. C spotted in 1703 don’t just provide us

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:04.740
with every fifth breath we take, they also
help make our cat litter more absorbent.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:14.500
And also, they’re just really nice to
look at.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:22.080
So thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.640
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.700
check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:31.580
And if you want to see more from us,

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:35.180
there's always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

